      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the weight of one bowling ball given the relationship between the weight of bowling balls and canoes. Let's denote the weight of one bowling ball by \\( B \\) and the weight of one canoe by \\( C \\).\n\nFirst, we know from the problem that seven identical bowling balls weigh the same as three identical canoes. This can be written as:\n\\[ 7B = 3C \\]\n\nWe are also given that two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds. Therefore, the weight of one canoe \\( C \\) is:\n\\[ C = \\frac{56}{2} = 28 \\text{ pounds} \\]\n\nNow, substituting the value of \\( C \\) into the first equation, we get:\n\\[ 7B = 3 \\times 28 \\]\n\\[ 7B = 84 \\]\nTo find \\( B \\), we divide both sides of the equation by 7:\n\\[ B = \\frac{84}{7} = 12 \\]\n\nThus, the weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
