      "text": "1. Let \\( b \\) be the weight of one bowling ball and \\( c \\) be the weight of one canoe.\n2. According to the problem, seven bowling balls weigh the same as three canoes. We can write this relationship as:\n   \\[\n   7b = 3c\n   \\]\n3. The problem also states that two canoes weigh a total of 56 pounds. We can write this as:\n   \\[\n   2c = 56\n   \\]\n4. Solving the equation \\( 2c = 56 \\) for \\( c \\):\n   \\[\n   c = \\frac{56}{2} = 28\n   \\]\n5. Now that we know the weight of one canoe, we can substitute \\( c = 28 \\) back into the equation \\( 7b = 3c \\) to find the weight of one bowling ball:\n   \\[\n   7b = 3 \\times 28\n   \\]\n6. Simplifying the right-hand side:\n   \\[\n   7b = 84\n   \\]\n7. Solving for \\( b \\) by dividing both sides by 7:\n   \\[\n   b = \\frac{84}{7} = 12\n   \\]\n\nConclusion: The weight of one bowling ball is \\(\\boxed{12}\\) pounds.",
